<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cenad</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Morisena</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cenad is a village in Timiș county, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, on the Mureș river near the Hungarian border. As Morisena it was the seat of the local ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahtum">Ahtum</a> around 1000; after his defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england">King Stephen</a> I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> it took the name Csanád from the victorious commander and in 1030 became the seat of a bishopric under Saint Gerard. Little remains of its medieval cathedral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cenad.jpg" alt="Cenad" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: rudolfmayer @ Mapillary.com · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cenad,_Timi%C8%99.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cenad lies in the west of Timiș County, on the left bank of the Mureș River, where it serves as a customs point on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. It is one of the places with the oldest documented history in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a>. People have lived there since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, about 7,000 years ago, and finds of the Starčevo–Criș, Vinča, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> and Tiszapolgár cultures show long settlement. The Roman era left pottery, tools, coins and bricks stamped by military units, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> camp of the Legio XIII Gemina stood on the site.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the site served as a temporary capital for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and was later ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>. Ahtum, who resided there at the beginning of the reign of Stephen I, brought architects from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> to build a church, a monastery and a palace. After his fall Morisena became a royal fortress and the seat of a county, and the bishop Gerard brought Italian engineers who built the fortress of Cenad on the site of the old one.</p><p>Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> visited it often, among them Ladislaus IV in 1278, Charles I in 1322 and Sigismund several times between 1394 and 1436. It was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> invasion of 1241 and rebuilt by Bishop Bulcsú Lád. After 1526 it passed to the Eastern Hungarian Kingdom and from 1551 to Ottoman rule as the centre of the Sanjak of Çanad, until the Austrian conquest of 1716. Its fortress was demolished under the terms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a>, with the work directed by General Oettingen.</p><p>German colonists arrived in 1764–1765 and founded German Cenad beside Serbian Cenad, and the two later merged. In 1858 the estate passed to the Aromanian Nakó family, who built a castle there. Serbian troops occupied the village in 1918, and it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> in 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cenad is a commune made up of a single village, formed in the 20th century by merging Cenadu Mare and Cenadu Vechi, with 3,537 inhabitants at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The ruins of the fortress and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahtum">Ahtum</a>'s monastery survive, and the neo-Gothic St. Gerard church of 1870 holds the sarcophagus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-csanad">Gerard of Csanád</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cenad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1000 he held the land between the Mureș, the Tisza and the Danube, ruling from <b>Morisena</b> (Cenad).</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-ahtum">Duchy of Ahtum</a></li></ul></section>
